);\nlabel(scale(0.6)*\"100\", (62, 98));\nlabel(scale(0.6)*\"110\", (95, 56));\nlabel(scale(0.6)*\"40\", (45, 15));\ndraw(circle((51, 18), 1));\ndraw(circle((70, 101), 1));\ndraw(circle((103, 59), 1));\n[/asy]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"adac204b53d631bad648b58416cd6b47f76231825d3b302893309ac87f796f1f"},"seconds":20.69013810157776,"seed":4069503175012489,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the interior degree measure of angle \\(A\\), we need to use the information given about the other angles in the polygon and the properties of polygons.\n\nGiven the figure, we have three angles labeled: 100°, 110°, and 40°. These angles are part of a quadrilateral, and we need to find the measure of angle \\(A\\).\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral is always 360°. This can be derived from the formula for the sum of interior angles of an \\(n\\)-sided polygon, which is \\((n-2) \\times 180°\\). For a quadrilateral (\\(n=4\\)), the sum is \\((4-2) \\times 180° = 2 \\times 180° = 360°\\).\n\nLet's denote the measure of angle \\(A\\) as \\(x\\). The sum of all the interior angles of the quadrilateral is:\n\\[\nx + 100° + 110° + 40° = 360°\n\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\nx + 250° = 360°\n\\]\nTo find \\(x\\), we subtract 250° from both sides of the equation:\n\\[\nx = 360° - 250° = 110°\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of angle \\(A\\) is \\(\\boxed{110}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{110}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
